// Class 12 Physics Unit IV: Electromagnetic Induction & Alternating Currents
// Comprehensive Gold Standard Reference Textbook (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora Standard) + Real-World Intuitions + All 9 Embedded High-Def Diagrams

export const c12Phy4HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit IV: Electromagnetic Induction &amp; Alternating Currents</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #0091EA; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>1. Magnetic Flux (&Phi;<sub>B</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Total number of magnetic field lines passing normally through a given surface area: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = <b>B</b> &sdot; <b>A</b> = B A cos&theta; [Weber Wb, Dimensions: [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #0091EA; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>2. Electromagnetic Induction (EMI):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Phenomenon of generating an induced electromotive force (EMF) and electric current in a closed circuit whenever the magnetic flux linked with it changes with time [Volt V, Dimensions: [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #0091EA; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>3. Self-Inductance (L):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Property of a coil that opposes any change in electric current flowing through itself by inducing an opposing back EMF (electrical inertia): &Epsilon; = -L(dI/dt) [Henry H, Dimensions: [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #0091EA; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>4. Mutual Inductance (M):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Property of a pair of magnetically coupled coils by which a changing current in the primary coil induces an EMF in the adjacent secondary coil: &Epsilon;<sub>2</sub> = -M(dI<sub>1</sub>/dt) [Henry H, Dimensions: [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #0091EA; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>5. Root Mean Square (RMS) Value (I<sub>rms</sub>, V<sub>rms</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Effective steady DC current/voltage that produces the exact same Joule heating in a given resistor as the AC does over one complete cycle: I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>0</sub> / &radic;2 &approx; 0.707 I<sub>0</sub> [Ampere A, Volt V].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #0091EA; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>6. Total Impedance (Z) &amp; Resonance:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Total effective opposition of an AC circuit: Z = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>] [&Omega;]. At resonance (X<sub>L</sub> = X<sub>C</sub>), impedance is minimum (Z = R) and current is maximum at f<sub>r</sub> = 1/[2&pi;&radic;(LC)].</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #0091EA; border-bottom: 2px solid #0091EA; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">PART A: ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 20px;\">1. Magnetic Flux &amp; Concept of Electromagnetic Induction</h3>\n<p><b>Historical Background &amp; Motivation:</b><br>In 1820, Oersted discovered that electric currents produce magnetic fields. For over a decade, scientists searched for the converse effect: <i>Can a magnetic field produce an electric current?</i> In 1831, English physicist <b>Michael Faraday</b> and American scientist <b>Joseph Henry</b> independently proved that electricity can indeed be generated by magnetic fields, but strictly on one vital condition: <b>the magnetic field linked with the conducting circuit must be changing with time</b>. This phenomenon of generating an electric current (or electromotive force) by altering the magnetic flux through a circuit is called <b>Electromagnetic Induction (EMI)</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Magnetic Flux as Rain Entering an Open Window</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Imagine holding an open rectangular window frame in falling rain. The total volume of rain entering through the window frame (&Phi;<sub>B</sub>) depends on: (1) How heavy the rain is (magnetic field intensity B), (2) The window opening area (A), and (3) The tilt angle of the window relative to the rain (&theta;). If the window frame is placed parallel to the rain drops (&theta; = 90&deg;), zero rain passes through!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Magnetic Flux (&Phi;<sub>B</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Total number of magnetic field lines passing normally through a given surface area: <b>&Phi;<sub>B</sub> = <b>B</b> &sdot; <b>A</b> = B A cos &theta;</b>, where &theta; is the angle between magnetic field <b>B</b> and the unit normal vector <b>n̂</b> to the surface.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Weber (Wb) = Tesla-meter<sup>2</sup> (T m<sup>2</sup>) = Volt-second (V s)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">2. Faraday's Laws of Electromagnetic Induction &amp; Induced EMF</h3>\n<p><b>Faraday's Classic Induction Experiments:</b><br>• <b>Experiment 1 (Magnet-Coil Movement):</b> When the North pole of a bar magnet is pushed into a stationary coil connected to a galvanometer, the needle deflects in one direction. When the magnet is pulled out, the needle deflects in the opposite direction. Moving the magnet faster produces a larger deflection. When the magnet is stationary inside the coil, the deflection is exactly zero.<br>• <b>Experiment 2 (Primary-Secondary Current Changes):</b> When current in a primary coil is switched on or off, a momentary induced current appears in an adjacent secondary coil.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 1: Faraday's Induction Experiments &amp; Induced EMF\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Faraday's Experiments & Induced EMF (Coil, N-S Magnet Motion IN and OUT, Galvanometer Deflection)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Faraday's Induction Experiments: Pushing magnet North pole IN increases flux, inducing current in one direction; pulling magnet OUT decreases flux, deflecting galvanometer in opposite direction (&Epsilon; = -N d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<p><b>Statements of Faraday's Laws:</b><br>1. <b>First Law (Qualitative):</b> Whenever the magnetic flux linked with a closed conducting circuit changes with time, an induced EMF is produced in the circuit. The induced EMF lasts only as long as the change in magnetic flux continues.<br>2. <b>Second Law (Quantitative):</b> The magnitude of the induced EMF is directly proportional to the time rate of change of magnetic flux linked with the circuit:<br><b>|&Epsilon;| = N <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b> (for a coil of N turns).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Induced Current & Induced Charge (I, &Delta;q)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Induced Current: <b>I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Epsilon;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Induced Charge: <b>&Delta;q = I &Delta;t = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> &Delta;&Phi;<sub>B</sub></b> (Charge depends ONLY on total flux change &Delta;&Phi;, completely independent of time &Delta;t!).</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Ampere (A), Coulomb (C)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[A<sup>1</sup>], [A<sup>1</sup> T<sup>1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.1); border: 1.2px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM TRAP &amp; CONCEPTUAL PITFALL: Induced Current vs Induced Charge Misconception</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FCA5A5; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Board exams frequently ask: 'If a bar magnet is pulled out of a coil slowly in 10 seconds vs rapidly in 0.1 second, how do induced EMF, induced current, and total induced charge compare?'<br>• Induced EMF &Epsilon; and current I are 100 times GREATER in the fast case (since d&Phi;/dt is 100x larger).<br>• Total Induced Charge &Delta;q = &Delta;&Phi;/R is strictly IDENTICAL in both cases because &Delta;q does NOT depend on time!</div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">3. Lenz's Law &amp; Principle of Conservation of Energy</h3>\n<p><b>Statement &amp; Direction of Induced EMF:</b><br>In 1834, Russian physicist <b>Heinrich Lenz</b> formulated the fundamental rule determining the polarity and direction of induced EMF. <b>Lenz's Law states:</b> <i>The direction of induced current in a closed circuit is always such that it opposes the very cause (the change in magnetic flux) that produces it.</i></p>\n<p>Incorporating Lenz's law into Faraday's mathematical equation yields:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>&Epsilon; = - N <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b></span> (where the negative sign explicitly signifies Lenz's opposition).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Lenz's Law as Nature's Electromagnetic Stubbornness</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Lenz's law is the electrical equivalent of inertia. If magnetic flux is increasing, the coil creates an opposing magnetic field to fight the increase. If magnetic flux is decreasing, the coil creates an aiding magnetic field to fight the decrease. The coil stubbornly resists ANY change in its magnetic state!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 2: Lenz's Law (Current Direction &amp; Conservation of Energy)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Lenz's Law (Current Direction & Opposing Induced B Field)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Lenz's Law in Action: Approaching North pole induces counter-clockwise current (I<sub>ind</sub>) creating an opposing B<sub>ind</sub> field to repel the incoming magnet; receding North pole induces clockwise current to attract and resist departure.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px;\">Proof: Lenz's Law is a Direct Consequence of Conservation of Energy</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Approaching North Pole:</b> When the North pole of a magnet is pushed towards a coil, Lenz's law dictates that the facing side develops a <b>North polarity</b> (counter-clockwise current). Like poles repel, so an opposing repulsive force pushes back against the incoming magnet.</li>\n      <li>2. <b>Mechanical Work &rarr; Electrical Energy:</b> The external agent must do mechanical work to overcome this repulsive force. This mechanical work done is converted into electrical energy in the coil, which eventually dissipates as Joule heat (I<sup>2</sup> R t).</li>\n      <li>3. <b>Receding North Pole:</b> When the North pole is pulled away from the coil, the facing side develops a <b>South polarity</b> (clockwise current). The attractive force pulls the magnet backward, resisting its withdrawal. Again, work must be done against attraction.</li>\n      <li>4. <i>Contradiction:</i> If Lenz's law were opposite (e.g. South polarity induced when N approaches), the magnet would be pulled inward with continuously increasing acceleration without any external work, creating free energy out of nothing and violating the First Law of Thermodynamics!</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">4. Motional EMF &amp; Eddy Currents (Foucault Currents)</h3>\n<p><b>Origin of Motional EMF:</b><br>When a conducting rod of length l moves with velocity <b>v</b> perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field <b>B</b>, the free conduction electrons inside the rod experience a magnetic Lorentz force <b>F</b><sub>m</sub> = - e (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>). This force drives free electrons towards one end, leaving the opposite end positively charged. An internal electrostatic field <b>E</b> builds up until electric force balances magnetic force: e E = e v B &rArr; E = v B. The induced motional potential difference across the ends is:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>&Epsilon; = E l = B l v</b></span>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Rotational Motional EMF of a Rotating Metallic Rod</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Physical Setup):</b> Consider a conducting rod of length l pivoted at one end O, rotating with constant angular velocity &omega; perpendicular to uniform magnetic field B.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Element Velocity):</b> Consider a small element of length dr at distance r from the pivot. Its linear speed is v = r &omega;.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Element EMF):</b> Induced EMF across element: d&Epsilon; = B v dr = B (r &omega;) dr.<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Integration from r = 0 to r = l):</b><br>\n    &Epsilon; = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>l</sup> B &omega; r dr = B &omega; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>]<sub>0</sub><sup>l</sup> = <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> B &omega; l<sup>2</sup> = B &pi; l<sup>2</sup> f</b></span> (where f = &omega; / 2&pi; is the frequency of revolution).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 15.5px;\">Eddy Currents (Foucault Currents): Applications &amp; Minimization</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li>• <b>Physical Origin:</b> When a bulk piece of metal moves through a non-uniform magnetic field, induced currents swirl in closed loops throughout the volume of the metal, creating strong braking forces (electromagnetic damping) and Joule heat.</li>\n      <li>• <b>Applications:</b> (1) Magnetic brakes in high-speed trains, (2) Induction furnaces for smelting metals, (3) Dead-beat galvanometers, (4) Induction cooktops.</li>\n      <li>• <b>Minimization in Transformer Cores:</b> Eddy current power loss is minimized by constructing cores out of <b>thin laminated sheets of soft iron</b> insulated from each other by varnish, which breaks large swirling current loops into tiny paths of high electrical resistance.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">5. Self-Induction &amp; Self-Inductance of a Solenoid</h3>\n<p><b>Physical Concept of Electrical Inertia:</b><br>When electric current through a coil changes with time, the magnetic flux linked with the coil itself changes. By Faraday-Lenz law, an opposing induced EMF (termed <b>Back EMF</b>) is generated in the same coil that resists any increase or decrease in current. This phenomenon is called <b>Self-Induction</b>, and self-inductance L represents the <b>electrical inertia</b> of the circuit (analogous to mechanical mass m).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Self-Inductance (Coefficient of Self-Induction) (L)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Total flux linkage: <b>N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = L I</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Induced Back EMF: <b>&Epsilon; = - L <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dI</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>. One Henry is the self-inductance of a coil in which a rate of current change of 1 A/s induces an opposing back EMF of 1 Volt.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Henry (H) = Wb A<sup>-1</sup> = V s A<sup>-1</sup> = &Omega; s</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 3: Self-Induction Circuit &amp; Back EMF Dynamics\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Self-Induction Circuit (Solenoid L, Switch, Back EMF, Growth/Decay Curve)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Self-Induction Circuit: When switch closes, changing current induces opposing Back EMF (&Epsilon; = -L dI/dt) in Solenoid L, slowing current growth and decay (electrical inertia).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Self-Inductance of a Long Solenoid</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1:</b> Consider a long solenoid of length l, cross-sectional area A, and total turns N (turns per unit length n = N / l).<br>\n    <b>Step 2:</b> When current I flows, uniform internal magnetic field is B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N / l) I.<br>\n    <b>Step 3:</b> Magnetic flux through each turn: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A = [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N / l) I] A.<br>\n    <b>Step 4:</b> Total flux linkage for N turns:<br>\n    N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = N [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N / l) I A] = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>] I.<br>\n    Comparing with N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = L I yields: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>L = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &mu;<sub>r</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l</b></span> (with core permeability &mu;<sub>r</sub>).<br>\n    <b>Magnetic Energy Stored in Inductor:</b> <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>U<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> L I<sup>2</sup></b></span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Magnetic Energy Density: <b>u<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span></b>.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">6. Mutual Induction &amp; Mutual Inductance of Coaxial Solenoids</h3>\n<p><b>Concept of Mutual Induction:</b><br>When current in a primary coil (P) changes with time, the changing magnetic flux passes through a neighboring secondary coil (S), inducing an EMF in the secondary circuit. This phenomenon of generating an induced EMF in one coil due to changing current in an adjacent coil is called <b>Mutual Induction</b> (the core principle of electrical transformers!).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Mutual Inductance (M)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Secondary flux linkage: <b>N<sub>2</sub> &Phi;<sub>21</sub> = M I<sub>1</sub></b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Induced secondary EMF: <b>&Epsilon;<sub>2</sub> = - M <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dI<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>. Reciprocity theorem establishes M<sub>12</sub> = M<sub>21</sub> = M.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Henry (H)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 4: Mutual Induction Setup &amp; Coaxial Solenoids\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Mutual Induction Setup (Primary Coil N1, Secondary Coil N2, Coaxial Core)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Mutual Induction Setup: Primary coil N<sub>1</sub> carries changing current, producing magnetic flux linked through coaxial core to Secondary coil N<sub>2</sub>, generating induced EMF (&Epsilon;<sub>2</sub> = -M dI<sub>1</sub>/dt) measured on Galvanometer.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Mutual Inductance of Two Long Coaxial Solenoids</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1:</b> Consider two long coaxial solenoids S<sub>1</sub> (inner, radius r<sub>1</sub>, turns N<sub>1</sub>) and S<sub>2</sub> (outer, radius r<sub>2</sub>, turns N<sub>2</sub>) of common length l.<br>\n    <b>Step 2:</b> Current I<sub>2</sub> in outer solenoid S<sub>2</sub> produces internal magnetic field B<sub>2</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sub>2</sub> I<sub>2</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub> / l) I<sub>2</sub>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3:</b> Flux linked with inner solenoid S<sub>1</sub> (area A<sub>1</sub> = &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>):<br>\n    N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = N<sub>1</sub> [B<sub>2</sub> A<sub>1</sub>] = N<sub>1</sub> [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub> / l) I<sub>2</sub> (&pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>)] = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>] I<sub>2</sub>.<br>\n    <b>Step 4:</b> Comparing with N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = M I<sub>2</sub> gives: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>M = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> A<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sub>1</sub> n<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> l</b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Coupling Coefficient:</b> <b>k = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(L<sub>1</sub> L<sub>2</sub>)</span></span></b> (where 0 &le; k &le; 1; k = 1 for perfectly coupled coils).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #0091EA; border-bottom: 2px solid #0091EA; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">PART B: ALTERNATING CURRENT (AC)</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 20px;\">7. Alternating Currents: Peak, Mean &amp; RMS (Root Mean Square) Values</h3>\n<p><b>Sinusoidal Nature of Alternating Voltage &amp; Current:</b><br>An alternating voltage/current is an electrical quantity whose magnitude varies periodically with time and whose polarity reverses at regular intervals: <b>V(t) = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</b> and <b>I(t) = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</b>, where V<sub>0</sub>, I<sub>0</sub> are peak amplitudes, &omega; = 2&pi;f is angular frequency, and f is supply frequency (50 Hz in India/Europe, 60 Hz in USA).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 5: Sinusoidal AC Waveform (Peak Voltage V<sub>0</sub> &amp; RMS Voltage V<sub>rms</sub>)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"AC Waveform (Peak Value V0, RMS Value Vrms, Time Period T)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Sinusoidal AC Waveform: Illustrating peak amplitude V<sub>0</sub>, effective Root Mean Square voltage V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub> / &radic;2 &approx; 0.707 V<sub>0</sub>, and full cycle period T = 1/f.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: RMS (Root Mean Square / Virtual / Effective) Value of AC</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Physical Definition):</b> The RMS value of AC is defined as that steady direct current (DC) which, when flowing through a given resistor for a given time, produces the EXACT SAME amount of Joule heat as the AC does in the same resistor during one complete cycle.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Heat in Time dt):</b> dH = I<sup>2</sup> R dt = (I<sub>0</sub> sin &omega;t)<sup>2</sup> R dt = I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R sin<sup>2</sup>(&omega;t) dt.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Total Heat over One Cycle T):</b> Using identity sin<sup>2</sup>&theta; = (1 - cos 2&theta;) / 2:<br>\n    H = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 - cos 2&omega;t</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>] dt = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> [t - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin 2&omega;t</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&omega;</span></span>]<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Equating to Equivalent DC Heat H = I<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> R T):</b><br>\n    I<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> R T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>I<sub>rms</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;2</span></span> &approx; 0.707 I<sub>0</sub></b></span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>V<sub>rms</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;2</span></span> &approx; 0.707 V<sub>0</sub></b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Mean Value over Half Cycle:</b> <b>I<sub>avg</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;</span></span> &approx; 0.637 I<sub>0</sub></b> (Over full cycle, I<sub>avg</sub> = 0).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.1); border: 1.2px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM TRAP &amp; CONCEPTUAL PITFALL: Why AC 220 V is More Dangerous than DC 220 V</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FCA5A5; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">A domestic 220 V AC supply represents the RMS value (V<sub>rms</sub> = 220 V). Its peak voltage reaches <b>V<sub>0</sub> = &radic;2 &times; 220 V &approx; 311.1 Volts!</b> Thus, a 220 V AC line fluctuates between +311 V and -311 V, creating far more severe electrical shock than a steady 220 V DC source.</div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">8. AC Circuits with Pure Elements: Resistor (R), Inductor (L) &amp; Capacitor (C)</h3>\n<p><b>Phase Relationships in Pure Components:</b><br>When sinusoidal voltage V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) is applied across individual passive elements, the current exhibits distinctive phase behaviors:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 15px;\">Comparison of Pure AC Circuits:</b>\n    <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <table style=\"width: 100%; min-width: 540px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.5;\">\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25); color: #00E5FF;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Circuit Element</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Current Equation</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Phase Difference (&phi;)</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Opposition / Reactance</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Behavior for DC (f = 0)</th>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>Pure Resistor (R)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">In Phase (&phi; = 0&deg;)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Resistance R (&Omega;)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Offers same resistance R</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>Pure Inductor (L)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t - &pi;/2)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Current <b>Lags</b> by 90&deg;</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>X<sub>L</sub> = &omega;L = 2&pi;fL</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">X<sub>L</sub> = 0 (Passes DC freely)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>Pure Capacitor (C)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t + &pi;/2)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Current <b>Leads</b> by 90&deg;</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>X<sub>C</sub> = 1/&omega;C = 1/(2&pi;fC)</b></td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">X<sub>C</sub> = &infin; (<b>Blocks DC completely!</b>)</td>\n        </tr>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">9. LCR Series Circuit (Phasor Diagram Treatment) &amp; Total Impedance</h3>\n<p><b>Phasor Analysis of Series LCR Network:</b><br>Consider a resistor R, an inductor L, and a capacitor C connected in series across an alternating voltage source V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t). Since components are in series, the exact same instantaneous current I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t &plusmn; &phi;) flows through all three elements.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 6: Series LCR Circuit Phasor Diagram\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"LCR Phasor Diagram (VR in phase, VL leads by 90 deg, VC lags by 90 deg)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Series LCR Phasor Diagram: Current I is reference, V<sub>R</sub> = I R is in-phase, V<sub>L</sub> = I X<sub>L</sub> leads by 90&deg;, V<sub>C</sub> = I X<sub>C</sub> lags by 90&deg;, and V<sub>total</sub> forms resultant vector at phase angle &phi;.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 7: Impedance Triangle (Resistance R, Reactance X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub> &amp; Impedance Z)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Impedance Triangle (Adjacent R, Opposite XL - XC, Hypotenuse Z, Phase Angle phi)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Impedance Triangle: Right-angled relationship showing Base = Resistance (R), Perpendicular = Net Reactance (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>), Hypotenuse = Total Impedance Z = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>], and Power Factor cos&phi; = R / Z.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Phasor Derivation of LCR Series Circuit Impedance</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Voltage Phasors):</b> Let current phasor <b>I</b> be along reference x-axis.<br>\n    • Resistor voltage <b>V<sub>R</sub> = I R</b> is in phase with <b>I</b> (along x-axis).<br>\n    • Inductor voltage <b>V<sub>L</sub> = I X<sub>L</sub></b> leads current by 90&deg; (along +y axis).<br>\n    • Capacitor voltage <b>V<sub>C</sub> = I X<sub>C</sub></b> lags current by 90&deg; (along -y axis).<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Net Reactive Voltage):</b> Net reactive voltage is (V<sub>L</sub> - V<sub>C</sub>) along +y axis (assuming V<sub>L</sub> &gt; V<sub>C</sub>).<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Resultant Applied Voltage V<sub>0</sub>):</b> By Pythagorean theorem on the phasor right triangle:<br>\n    V<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = V<sub>R</sub><sup>2</sup> + (V<sub>L</sub> - V<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup> = (I<sub>0</sub> R)<sup>2</sup> + (I<sub>0</sub> X<sub>L</sub> - I<sub>0</sub> X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup> = I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> [R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>].<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Total Impedance Z &amp; Phase Angle &phi;):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>Z = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub></span></span> = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (&omega;L - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;C</span></span>)<sup>2</sup>]</b></span>.<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>tan &phi; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>L</sub> - V<sub>C</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>R</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;L - 1/&omega;C</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span></b></span>.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">10. Electrical Resonance in LCR Series Circuit &amp; Quality Factor (Q)</h3>\n<p><b>Series Resonance Phenomenon:</b><br>An LCR series circuit is said to be in <b>Electrical Resonance</b> when the supply angular frequency &omega; reaches a critical value where inductive reactance equals capacitive reactance: <b>X<sub>L</sub> = X<sub>C</sub> &rArr; &omega;<sub>r</sub> L = 1 / (&omega;<sub>r</sub> C)</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 8: Series LCR Resonance Curve &amp; Bandwidth (Selectivity)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Resonance Curve (Current I vs Frequency f, Peak Imax, Half Power Frequencies f1, f2, Bandwidth)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Series LCR Resonance Curve: Shows current I peaking at resonant frequency f<sub>0</sub> = 1/[2&pi;&radic;(LC)] where Z<sub>min</sub> = R. At half-power frequencies f<sub>1</sub> and f<sub>2</sub>, current falls to I<sub>max</sub> / &radic;2, defining circuit Bandwidth &Delta;f = f<sub>2</sub> - f<sub>1</sub> and Quality Factor Q = f<sub>0</sub> / Bandwidth.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Resonant Frequency & Quality Factor (f<sub>r</sub>, Q)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Resonant Frequency: <b>f<sub>r</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi;&radic;(LC)</span></span></b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Quality Factor: <b>Q = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;<sub>r</sub> L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> &radic;<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">C</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">f<sub>r</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Bandwidth (2&Delta;f)</span></span></b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Hertz (Hz), Dimensionless</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[T<sup>-1</sup>], [1]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 15.5px;\">Crucial Characteristics of Series Resonance:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Impedance is Minimum:</b> Z<sub>min</sub> = R (Circuit is purely resistive).</li>\n      <li>2. <b>Current is Maximum:</b> I<sub>max</sub> = V / R (Acceptor Circuit used in Radio/TV antenna tuners!).</li>\n      <li>3. <b>Phase Angle &phi; = 0:</b> Voltage and current are perfectly in phase; Power Factor <b>cos&phi; = 1</b>.</li>\n      <li>4. <b>Q-Factor (Selectivity):</b> Higher Q means narrower bandwidth 2&Delta;f and sharper resonance curve, giving clean separation between radio stations.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">11. Power in AC Circuits, Power Factor &amp; Wattless Current</h3>\n<p><b>Instantaneous and Average Electrical Power:</b><br>In an AC circuit with voltage V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) and current I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t - &phi;), instantaneous power is p(t) = V(t) I(t). Integrating over a full period gives the <b>Average True Power</b>:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>P<sub>avg</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> I<sub>rms</sub> cos &phi; = (Apparent Power) &times; (Power Factor)</b></span>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Power Factor (cos &phi;)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Ratio of true active power to apparent power: <b>cos &phi; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Z</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>]</span></span></b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Dimensionless (0 to 1)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[1]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Wattless Current as a Bouncing Basketball</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">When you bounce a basketball on a hard floor, energy transfers back and forth between your hand and the ball without permanently losing energy to heat. Similarly, in a pure inductor or capacitor, energy charges into the magnetic/electric field during one quarter-cycle and returns completely to the source in the next. The current flows (I_rms sin&phi;), but average energy consumed is ZERO (Wattless Current)!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 15.5px;\">Choke Coil &amp; Wattless Current Control:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li>• A <b>Choke Coil</b> consists of thick copper wire wound over a soft iron core, providing very high inductance L and nearly zero resistance R (R &approx; 0).</li>\n      <li>• Power factor cos&phi; = R / &radic;(R<sup>2</sup> + &omega;<sup>2</sup> L<sup>2</sup>) &approx; 0 &rArr; Average power dissipated P<sub>avg</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> I<sub>rms</sub> cos&phi; &approx; 0.</li>\n      <li>• It limits and controls AC current in fluorescent tube lights without wasting electrical energy as heat (unlike a rheostat resistor which wastes I<sup>2</sup> R power!).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">12. AC Generator (Alternator) Construction &amp; Working Theory</h3>\n<p><b>Principle of AC Generator:</b><br>An AC Generator converts mechanical energy into alternating electrical energy based on the principle of <b>Electromagnetic Induction</b>. When a closed armature coil is mechanically rotated with uniform angular velocity &omega; inside a strong magnetic field B, the magnetic flux linked with the coil continuously changes, generating an alternating sinusoidal EMF.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 9: AC Generator (Alternator) &amp; Electrical Transformer Principles\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"AC Generator (Magnets, Coil, Armature, Slip Rings, Brushes) & Transformer (Step-up and Step-down)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 AC Generator & Transformer: (Left) AC Generator converts mechanical rotation of armature coil between magnetic poles into sinusoidal AC via slip rings & brushes (&Epsilon; = N B A &omega; sin&omega;t); (Right) Step-up (N<sub>s</sub> > N<sub>p</sub>) and Step-down (N<sub>s</sub> < N<sub>p</sub>) Transformers stepping voltages via mutual induction (N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub> = V<sub>s</sub>/V<sub>p</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Theory of AC Generator Induced EMF</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Flux Linkage at time t):</b> When a coil of N turns and area A rotates at angular speed &omega;, at time t the angle between magnetic field <b>B</b> and coil normal <b>n̂</b> is &theta; = &omega;t.<br>\n    &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A cos(&omega;t).<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Applying Faraday's Law):</b><br>\n    &Epsilon; = - N <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = - N <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> [B A cos(&omega;t)] = - N B A (- &omega; sin &omega;t).<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>&Epsilon; = N B A &omega; sin(&omega;t) = &Epsilon;<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</b></span>,<br>\n    where peak EMF is <b>&Epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = N B A &omega; = 2&pi; f N B A</b>.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 30px;\">13. Electrical Transformer: Principle, Construction, Working &amp; Energy Losses</h3>\n<p><b>Principle &amp; Construction:</b><br>A Transformer is a static electromagnetic device used for stepping up or stepping down alternating voltages without altering the supply frequency, operating on the principle of <b>Mutual Induction</b> between two electrically isolated coils wound on a common laminated soft iron core.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Transformer Equation &amp; Transformation Ratio (K)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1:</b> Let an alternating voltage V<sub>p</sub> be applied across primary coil of N<sub>p</sub> turns. The alternating flux &Phi;<sub>B</sub> linked with each turn induces back EMF in primary: V<sub>p</sub> = - N<sub>p</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub> / dt).<br>\n    <b>Step 2:</b> Assuming zero flux leakage, the same flux &Phi;<sub>B</sub> links with secondary coil of N<sub>s</sub> turns: V<sub>s</sub> = - N<sub>s</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub> / dt).<br>\n    <b>Step 3:</b> Dividing the equations gives: <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>p</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>p</sub></span></span> = K</b></span> (Transformation Ratio).<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Ideal Transformer with 100% Efficiency):</b> Input Power = Output Power &rArr; V<sub>p</sub> I<sub>p</sub> = V<sub>s</sub> I<sub>s</sub> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>p</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>s</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>p</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>p</sub></span></span> = K</b></span>.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #0091EA; font-size: 15px;\">Master Table: 5 Major Energy Losses in Real Transformers &amp; Remedies</b>\n    <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <table style=\"width: 100%; min-width: 540px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.5;\">\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.25); color: #00E5FF;\"><th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Loss Name</th><th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Physical Cause</th><th style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; text-align: left;\">Engineering Remedy</th></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>1. Copper Loss (I<sup>2</sup>R)</b></td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Joule heating in copper windings</td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Use thick copper wires of low resistance</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>2. Iron / Eddy Loss</b></td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Circulating eddy currents in soft iron core</td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Use <b>laminated core</b> insulated with varnish</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>3. Hysteresis Loss</b></td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Energy lost in cyclic magnetization of core</td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Use soft iron core with <b>narrow hysteresis loop</b></td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>4. Flux Leakage</b></td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Not all primary flux links with secondary</td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Wind primary and secondary coils coaxially</td></tr>\n        <tr><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>5. Humming Loss</b></td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Magnetostriction vibration of core</td><td style=\"padding: 6px 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Tightly clamp and bolt core laminations</td></tr>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-top: 35px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet</h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-top: 14px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Magnetic Flux:</b> &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A cos&theta;</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Faraday-Lenz:</b> &Epsilon; = - N (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub> / dt)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Motional EMF:</b> &Epsilon; = B l v &nbsp;|&nbsp; &Epsilon;<sub>rot</sub> = 1/2 B &omega; l<sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Solenoid Inductance:</b> L = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Magnetic Energy:</b> U<sub>B</sub> = 1/2 L I<sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Mutual Solenoids:</b> M = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sub>1</sub> n<sub>2</sub> A<sub>1</sub> l</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>RMS AC:</b> I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>0</sub> / &radic;2, V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub> / &radic;2</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Reactances:</b> X<sub>L</sub> = &omega;L, X<sub>C</sub> = 1 / (&omega;C)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>LCR Impedance:</b> Z = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>]</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Resonance:</b> f<sub>r</sub> = 1 / [2&pi;&radic;(LC)]</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>AC Power:</b> P<sub>avg</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> I<sub>rms</sub> cos&phi;</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Transformer Ratio:</b> V<sub>s</sub>/V<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub> = I<sub>p</sub>/I<sub>s</sub> = K</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

export const c12Phy4HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #FFFFFF; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #0091EA; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📝 Comprehensive Board Question Bank &amp; Step-by-Step Solutions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 14px; margin: 0; text-align: center !important;\">Unit IV: Electromagnetic Induction &amp; Alternating Currents &bull; Exactly 100 Graduated Questions (Sections A, B, C)</p>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #0091EA; border-bottom: 2px solid #0091EA; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">SECTION A: VERY SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS (1 MARK EACH &ndash; Q1 TO Q40)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q1: Define magnetic flux and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Magnetic flux (&Phi;<sub>B</sub>) linked with a surface is defined as the total number of magnetic field lines crossing normally through that surface: <b>&Phi;<sub>B</sub> = <b>B</b> &sdot; <b>A</b> = B A cos &theta;</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>Weber (Wb)</b> [1 Wb = 1 Tesla &times; 1 m<sup>2</sup> = 1 V s].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q2: State the dimensional formula of magnetic flux.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      From &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B &middot; A = [F / (q v)] &middot; A = [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> / (A<sup>1</sup> T<sup>1</sup> &middot; L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>)] &middot; [L<sup>2</sup>] = <b>[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q3: Under what orientation is the magnetic flux linked with a surface maximum?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Magnetic flux is maximum when the magnetic field <b>B</b> is perpendicular to the surface of the coil (i.e. angle &theta; between <b>B</b> and unit normal area vector <b>n̂</b> is &theta; = 0&deg;): <b>&Phi;<sub>max</sub> = B A cos 0&deg; = B A</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q4: Under what orientation is the magnetic flux through a coil zero?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Magnetic flux is zero when the magnetic field <b>B</b> is parallel to the plane of the coil (i.e. angle &theta; between <b>B</b> and unit normal area vector <b>n̂</b> is &theta; = 90&deg;): <b>&Phi; = B A cos 90&deg; = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q5: State Faraday's first law of electromagnetic induction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Faraday's First Law states that whenever the magnetic flux linked with a closed conducting circuit changes with time, an induced electromotive force (EMF) is produced in the circuit, which lasts only as long as the change in flux continues.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q6: State Faraday's second law of electromagnetic induction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Faraday's Second Law states that the magnitude of the induced EMF in a circuit is directly proportional to the time rate of change of magnetic flux linked with it: <b>|&Epsilon;| = N <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q7: State Lenz's law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Lenz's Law states that the direction of induced current in a circuit is always such that it opposes the very cause (the change in magnetic flux) that produces it.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q8: Which fundamental conservation law is the basis of Lenz's law?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Lenz's law is a direct consequence of the <b>Law of Conservation of Energy</b>. The mechanical work done by an external agent against opposing magnetic forces is converted into electrical energy in the coil.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q9: Why does a metallic ring tossed upward over an active AC electromagnet fly into the air (Thomson's jumping ring)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The alternating magnetic field induces large eddy currents in the metallic ring. By Lenz's law, the magnetic field produced by the induced eddy currents opposes the primary field, creating a continuous upward repulsive electromagnetic force that launches the ring.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q10: Define one Weber (1 Wb) of magnetic flux.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      One Weber is the magnetic flux produced across a surface of area 1 square meter placed perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of 1 Tesla (1 Wb = 1 T &times; 1 m<sup>2</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q11: What is the formula for the charge &Delta;q induced in a circuit of resistance R when flux changes by &Delta;&Phi;?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The total induced charge is <b>&Delta;q = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span></b>. It depends only on total flux change &Delta;&Phi;<sub>B</sub> and resistance R, completely independent of the time taken for the change.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q12: State Fleming's Right-Hand Rule.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of the right hand mutually perpendicular to each other. If the <b>forefinger</b> points in the direction of the magnetic field and the <b>thumb</b> points in the direction of motion of the conductor, then the <b>middle finger</b> points in the direction of the <b>induced current</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q13: Define Motional EMF.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Motional EMF is the electromotive force induced across the ends of a conducting rod when it moves through a magnetic field due to the magnetic Lorentz force acting on its free conduction electrons: <b>&Epsilon; = B l v</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q14: What is the induced EMF across a conducting rod of length l moving parallel to a magnetic field B?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Zero (&Epsilon; = 0), because the velocity vector <b>v</b> is parallel to <b>B</b> (&theta; = 0&deg;), so the magnetic Lorentz force <b>F</b><sub>m</sub> = q(<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>) = 0.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q15: What are Eddy Currents (Foucault Currents)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Eddy currents are closed loops of induced electrical current circulating throughout the bulk volume of a solid conducting metal when it experiences a changing magnetic flux.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q16: Why are transformer cores laminated?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Transformer cores are constructed from thin laminated soft iron sheets insulated with lacquer/varnish to increase electrical resistance along eddy current paths, thereby drastically reducing energy losses due to eddy current heating (I<sup>2</sup> R).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q17: Name any two devices that utilize eddy currents beneficially.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Magnetic Brakes</b> in high-speed trains.<br>2. <b>Induction Furnaces</b> for melting high-temperature alloys.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q18: Define self-induction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Self-induction is the phenomenon by which an opposing induced EMF (back EMF) is produced in a coil itself whenever the electric current flowing through that coil changes with time.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q19: Define Self-Inductance (L) and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Self-inductance L of a coil is defined as the magnetic flux linkage per unit electric current: <b>L = N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> / I</b>, or as the back EMF induced per unit rate of decrease of current: <b>L = - &Epsilon; / (dI/dt)</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>Henry (H)</b> [1 H = 1 V s A<sup>-1</sup> = 1 Wb A<sup>-1</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q20: State the dimensional formula of self-inductance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      From &Epsilon; = L (dI/dt) &rArr; [L] = [&Epsilon; &middot; dt / dI] = [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup> &middot; T<sup>1</sup> / A<sup>1</sup>] = <b>[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q21: Define one Henry (1 H) of self-inductance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A coil has a self-inductance of 1 Henry if an opposing back EMF of 1 Volt is induced in it when the electric current changes at the rate of 1 Ampere per second.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q22: Write the formula for the self-inductance of a long straight air-cored solenoid.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>L = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l</b>, where N is total turns, l is length, A is cross-sectional area, and n = N/l is turns per unit length.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q23: How does the self-inductance of a solenoid change if an iron core of relative permeability &mu;<sub>r</sub> is inserted inside it?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The self-inductance increases by a factor of &mu;<sub>r</sub>: <b>L<sub>iron</sub> = &mu;<sub>r</sub> L<sub>air</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q24: Write the expression for magnetic energy stored in an inductor of inductance L carrying current I.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The stored magnetic potential energy is <b>U<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> L I<sup>2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q25: State the expression for the magnetic energy density u<sub>B</sub> in a magnetic field B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The magnetic energy density (energy per unit volume) in vacuum is <b>u<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span></b> [J m<sup>-3</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q26: Define mutual induction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Mutual induction is the phenomenon of producing an induced EMF in a secondary coil when the electric current in an adjacent primary coil changes with time.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q27: Define Mutual Inductance (M) and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Mutual inductance M is defined as the total magnetic flux linked with the secondary coil per unit current in the primary coil: <b>M = N<sub>2</sub> &Phi;<sub>21</sub> / I<sub>1</sub></b>, or <b>M = - &Epsilon;<sub>2</sub> / (dI<sub>1</sub>/dt)</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>Henry (H)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q28: What is the maximum value of the coupling coefficient k between two coils?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The maximum value of the coupling coefficient is <b>k = 1</b>, corresponding to perfect magnetic coupling where 100% of the primary flux links with the secondary coil (M = &radic;(L<sub>1</sub> L<sub>2</sub>)).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q29: What is alternating current (AC)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Alternating current is an electric current whose magnitude varies periodically with time and whose direction reverses periodically at regular intervals: I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q30: What is the frequency of domestic AC supply in India?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The standard domestic AC supply frequency in India is <b>50 Hz</b> (50 cycles per second, reversing direction 100 times per second), with an RMS voltage of <b>220 V</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q31: State the relationship between peak value I<sub>0</sub> and RMS value I<sub>rms</sub> of AC.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>I<sub>rms</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;2</span></span> &approx; 0.707 I<sub>0</sub></b> (or I<sub>0</sub> = &radic;2 I<sub>rms</sub> &approx; 1.414 I<sub>rms</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q32: What is the average value of alternating current over one complete cycle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The average (mean) value of AC over one full cycle is strictly <b>Zero (I<sub>avg</sub> = 0)</b> because the positive half-cycle is exactly canceled by the equal and opposite negative half-cycle.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q33: Define inductive reactance X<sub>L</sub> and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Inductive reactance is the opposition offered by a pure inductor to the flow of alternating current: <b>X<sub>L</sub> = &omega; L = 2&pi; f L</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>Ohm (&Omega;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q34: How does inductive reactance X<sub>L</sub> vary with AC frequency f?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Inductive reactance is directly proportional to frequency: <b>X<sub>L</sub> &prop; f</b>. For DC (f = 0), X<sub>L</sub> = 0 (an inductor offers zero reactance to DC).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q35: Define capacitive reactance X<sub>C</sub> and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Capacitive reactance is the opposition offered by a pure capacitor to the flow of alternating current: <b>X<sub>C</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega; C</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; f C</span></span></b>. Its SI unit is the <b>Ohm (&Omega;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q36: Why does a capacitor block direct current (DC)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For DC, the frequency is f = 0. Therefore, capacitive reactance becomes <b>X<sub>C</sub> = 1 / (2&pi; &times; 0 &times; C) = &infin; (infinite)</b>. Hence, a capacitor acts as an open circuit and completely blocks DC.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q37: What is the phase difference between voltage and current in a pure inductor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In a pure inductor, the alternating current <b>lags</b> behind the alternating voltage by a phase angle of <b>90&deg; (&pi;/2 radians)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q38: What is the phase difference between voltage and current in a pure capacitor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In a pure capacitor, the alternating current <b>leads</b> ahead of the alternating voltage by a phase angle of <b>90&deg; (&pi;/2 radians)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q39: Define Impedance (Z) of an AC circuit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Impedance is the total effective opposition offered by a combination of resistance, inductance, and capacitance to the flow of alternating current: <b>Z = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>]</b>. Its SI unit is the <b>Ohm (&Omega;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q40: What is Wattless Current?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Wattless current (idle current) is that component of alternating current (I<sub>rms</sub> sin &phi;) which is in quadrature (90&deg; out of phase) with the alternating voltage, and consumes strictly <b>Zero average electrical power (P<sub>avg</sub> = 0)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #0091EA; border-bottom: 2px solid #0091EA; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS (2 &amp; 3 MARKS EACH &ndash; Q41 TO Q80)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q41: A square loop of side 10 cm and resistance 0.5 &Omega; is placed vertically in the east-west plane. A uniform magnetic field of 0.10 T is set up across the plane in the north-east direction. The magnetic field is decreased to zero in 0.70 s at a steady rate. Determine the magnitude of induced EMF and current.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Magnetic Flux:</b> Area A = (0.1 m)<sup>2</sup> = 0.01 m<sup>2</sup>. Angle &theta; = 45&deg; (between field in NE direction and normal to EW plane).<br>&Phi;<sub>initial</sub> = B A cos 45&deg; = 0.10 &times; 0.01 &times; (1/&radic;2) = 10<sup>-3</sup> / &radic;2 = 0.707 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> Wb.<br>&Phi;<sub>final</sub> = 0.<br><b>2. Induced EMF:</b> &Epsilon; = |&Delta;&Phi; / &Delta;t| = (0.707 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) / 0.70 = <b>1.01 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> V (1.01 mV)</b>.<br><b>3. Induced Current:</b> I = &Epsilon; / R = (1.01 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) / 0.5 = <b>2.02 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> A (2.02 mA)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q42: Derive the expression for the motional EMF induced in a straight conductor of length l moving with velocity v perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Lorentz Force on Free Electrons:</b> Each electron of charge -e moving with velocity <b>v</b> in magnetic field <b>B</b> experiences downward magnetic force: F<sub>m</sub> = e v B.<br>2. <b>Electrostatic Field Setup:</b> Accumulation of electrons at one end creates an internal electric field <b>E</b> exerting upward force F<sub>e</sub> = e E.<br>3. <b>Equilibrium:</b> e E = e v B &rArr; E = v B.<br>4. <b>Induced EMF:</b> &Epsilon; = E &middot; l = <b>B l v</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q43: A 1.0 m long metallic rod is rotated with an angular frequency of 400 rad/s about an axis normal to the rod passing through its one end. The other end of the rod is in contact with a circular metallic ring. A constant uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T parallel to the axis exists everywhere. Calculate the EMF developed between the center and the ring.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: &Epsilon; = 1/2 B &omega; l<sup>2</sup><br>Given: B = 0.5 T, &omega; = 400 rad/s, l = 1.0 m.<br>&Epsilon; = 1/2 &times; 0.5 &times; 400 &times; (1.0)<sup>2</sup> = <b>100 Volts</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q44: Explain why a copper plate swinging like a pendulum between the poles of a strong electromagnet comes to an abrupt halt when the magnet is turned on.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      As the solid copper plate enters and exits the strong magnetic field, the changing magnetic flux induces large swirling <b>Eddy Currents</b> within its volume. According to Lenz's law, the magnetic forces created by these eddy currents oppose the plate's motion (<b>Electromagnetic Damping</b>), converting the kinetic energy of the swinging plate into Joule heat and bringing it to an abrupt stop.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q45: How can the electromagnetic damping of a swinging copper plate be reduced?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      By cutting narrow slots (comb-like teeth) into the copper plate. The slots break the continuous metallic paths, drastically reducing the size and circulation of the eddy current loops, thereby reducing the magnetic braking force and allowing the plate to swing freely.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q46: Derive the formula for self-inductance of a long solenoid of length l, cross-sectional area A, and total turns N.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Uniform field inside solenoid: B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N/l) I.<br>2. Flux through each turn: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N/l) I A.<br>3. Total flux linkage: N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = N [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N/l) I A] = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>] I.<br>4. Comparing with N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = L I gives: <b>L = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q47: An air-cored solenoid has length 30 cm, area 25 cm<sup>2</sup>, and 500 turns. Find its self-inductance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: l = 0.30 m, A = 25 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>2</sup>, N = 500.<br>L = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A) / l = [(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; (500)<sup>2</sup> &times; (25 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup>)] / 0.30<br>L = [(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; (250000) &times; (2.5 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>)] / 0.30 = <b>2.62 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> H (2.62 mH)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q48: A current in a coil drops from 5.0 A to 0 A in 0.1 s. If an average back EMF of 200 V is induced, find the self-inductance of the coil.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: &Epsilon; = - L (dI / dt)<br>|&Epsilon;| = L |&Delta;I / &Delta;t| &rArr; 200 = L &times; |(0 - 5.0) / 0.1| = L &times; 50<br>L = 200 / 50 = <b>4.0 Henry</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q49: Derive the expression for the magnetic energy stored in an inductor U = 1/2 L I<sup>2</sup>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. When current grows from 0 to I, work must be done against back EMF: |&Epsilon;| = L (di/dt).<br>2. Work done in time dt: dW = P dt = |&Epsilon;| i dt = [L (di/dt)] i dt = L i di.<br>3. Total work done to establish steady current I:<br>W = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>I</sup> L i di = L [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">i<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>]<sub>0</sub><sup>I</sup> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> L I<sup>2</sup></b>.<br>This work is stored as magnetic potential energy U<sub>B</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q50: Derive the formula for mutual inductance of two coaxial solenoids.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Solenoid S<sub>2</sub> (outer, radius r<sub>2</sub>, turns N<sub>2</sub>) carries current I<sub>2</sub>: B<sub>2</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub>/l) I<sub>2</sub>.<br>2. Flux linked with inner solenoid S<sub>1</sub> (radius r<sub>1</sub>, turns N<sub>1</sub>):<br>N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = N<sub>1</sub> [B<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>] = N<sub>1</sub> [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub>/l) I<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>] = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>] I<sub>2</sub>.<br>3. Comparing with N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = M I<sub>2</sub>: <b>M = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q51: Distinguish between Self-Induction and Mutual Induction (3 points).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Coil Number:</b> Self-induction involves a single isolated coil; mutual induction involves two separate coupled coils (primary and secondary).<br>2. <b>Induced EMF Cause:</b> In self-induction, EMF is induced by changing current in the SAME coil; in mutual induction, EMF is induced by changing current in the ADJACENT coil.<br>3. <b>Application:</b> Self-induction is used in choke coils and inductive ballasts; mutual induction is the basis of transformers and ignition coils.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q52: Show mathematically that the average value of AC over a full cycle is zero, but over a positive half-cycle is 2 I<sub>0</sub> / &pi;.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Full Cycle:</b> I<sub>avg</sub> = (1/T) &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) dt = (I<sub>0</sub> / &omega;T) [-cos(&omega;t)]<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> = (I<sub>0</sub> / 2&pi;) [-cos 2&pi; + cos 0] = (I<sub>0</sub> / 2&pi;) [-1 + 1] = <b>0</b>.<br>2. <b>Positive Half-Cycle (0 to T/2):</b><br>I<sub>avg</sub> = [1 / (T/2)] &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T/2</sup> I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) dt = (2 I<sub>0</sub> / &omega;T) [-cos(&omega;t)]<sub>0</sub><sup>T/2</sup> = (2 I<sub>0</sub> / 2&pi;) [-cos &pi; + cos 0] = (I<sub>0</sub> / &pi;) [-(-1) + 1] = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&pi;</span></span> &approx; 0.637 I<sub>0</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q53: An AC voltage source is given by V = 282.8 sin(314 t). Find (a) Peak voltage, (b) RMS voltage, (c) Frequency of supply.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Comparing with V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t):<br>(a) Peak voltage: <b>V<sub>0</sub> = 282.8 Volts</b>.<br>(b) RMS voltage: V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub> / &radic;2 = 282.8 / 1.414 = <b>200 Volts</b>.<br>(c) Angular frequency: &omega; = 314 rad/s &rArr; Frequency f = &omega; / (2&pi;) = 314 / (2 &times; 3.14) = <b>50 Hz</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q54: Explain with a phasor diagram why current lags voltage by 90&deg; in a pure inductor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Applied Voltage:</b> V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t).<br>2. <b>Inductor Loop Equation:</b> V - L (di/dt) = 0 &rArr; di = (V/L) dt = (V<sub>0</sub>/L) sin(&omega;t) dt.<br>3. <b>Integration:</b> i = &int; (V<sub>0</sub>/L) sin(&omega;t) dt = - (V<sub>0</sub> / &omega;L) cos(&omega;t) = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t - &pi;/2).<br>4. <b>Conclusion:</b> The phase term (&omega;t - &pi;/2) proves that current <b>lags behind voltage by &pi;/2 radians (90&deg;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q55: Explain with a phasor diagram why current leads voltage by 90&deg; in a pure capacitor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Applied Voltage:</b> V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t).<br>2. <b>Charge on Capacitor:</b> q = C V = C V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t).<br>3. <b>Current:</b> i = dq/dt = C V<sub>0</sub> &omega; cos(&omega;t) = (&omega; C V<sub>0</sub>) sin(&omega;t + &pi;/2) = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t + &pi;/2).<br>4. <b>Conclusion:</b> The phase term (&omega;t + &pi;/2) proves that current <b>leads ahead of voltage by &pi;/2 radians (90&deg;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q56: A 100 &Omega; resistor is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz AC supply. (a) What is the RMS value of current? (b) What is the net power consumed over a full cycle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: R = 100 &Omega;, V<sub>rms</sub> = 220 V, f = 50 Hz.<br>(a) RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> / R = 220 / 100 = <b>2.20 A</b>.<br>(b) Net Power: P = V<sub>rms</sub> &times; I<sub>rms</sub> = 220 &times; 2.2 = <b>484 Watts</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q57: A 44 mH inductor is connected to 220 V, 50 Hz AC supply. Determine the RMS value of the current in the circuit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Inductive Reactance: X<sub>L</sub> = 2&pi; f L = 2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 50 &times; (44 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) = 13.82 &Omega;.<br>2. RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> / X<sub>L</sub> = 220 / 13.82 = <b>15.92 A</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q58: A 60 &mu;F capacitor is connected to a 110 V, 60 Hz AC supply. Determine the RMS value of the current in the circuit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Capacitive Reactance: X<sub>C</sub> = 1 / (2&pi; f C) = 1 / [2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 60 &times; (60 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>)] = 1 / (0.02262) = 44.21 &Omega;.<br>2. RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> / X<sub>C</sub> = 110 / 44.21 = <b>2.49 A</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q59: What is an acceptor circuit? Why is a series LCR circuit called an acceptor circuit?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A series LCR circuit offers minimum impedance (Z<sub>min</sub> = R) and draws maximum current at its resonant frequency f<sub>r</sub> = 1 / [2&pi;&radic;(LC)]. Because it readily accepts and amplifies signals of this particular resonant frequency while rejecting all other frequencies, it is called an <b>Acceptor Circuit</b> (used in radio receivers).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q60: What is the Quality Factor (Q-factor) of a resonant LCR circuit? What does it signify?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The Q-factor is the ratio of resonant frequency to bandwidth: <b>Q = &omega;<sub>r</sub> / (2&Delta;&omega;) = (1/R) &radic;(L/C)</b>. It measures the <b>sharpness of resonance</b> and the selectivity of the tuning circuit (higher Q means better selectivity and rejection of neighboring channels).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q61: A series LCR circuit has L = 2.0 H, C = 32 &mu;F, and R = 10 &Omega;. Determine the resonant frequency &omega;<sub>r</sub> and Q-factor.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Resonant Frequency: &omega;<sub>r</sub> = 1 / &radic;(LC) = 1 / &radic;[2.0 &times; 32 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>] = 1 / &radic;(64 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) = 1 / (8 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) = <b>125 rad/s</b>.<br>2. Q-Factor: Q = (&omega;<sub>r</sub> L) / R = (125 &times; 2.0) / 10 = <b>25</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q62: Define Power Factor in an AC circuit. What are its maximum and minimum values?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Power Factor is the cosine of the phase angle &phi; between voltage and current: <b>cos &phi; = R / Z</b>.<br>• Maximum value is <b>cos &phi; = 1</b> (in pure resistive circuit or series LCR at resonance).<br>• Minimum value is <b>cos &phi; = 0</b> (in pure inductive or pure capacitive circuit).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q63: What is a Choke Coil? Why is it preferred over an ordinary rheostat for dimming AC lights?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A Choke Coil is an inductor of high self-inductance L and very low resistance R (R &approx; 0). It reduces and controls AC current without wasting electrical energy as heat because its power factor cos&phi; &approx; 0 (P<sub>avg</sub> = 0). An ordinary rheostat wastes substantial power as I<sup>2</sup> R Joule heat.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q64: Explain the principle of an AC Generator.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      An AC Generator works on the principle of <b>Electromagnetic Induction</b>. When a closed rectangular coil of wire is mechanically rotated in a uniform magnetic field, the magnetic flux linked with the coil changes continuously, inducing an alternating sinusoidal electromotive force: &Epsilon; = N B A &omega; sin(&omega;t).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q65: What are slip rings in an AC generator, and what is their function?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Slip rings are two circular brass rings connected to the ends of the rotating armature coil that rotate continuously with the coil. Carbon brushes rest against the slip rings to collect the induced alternating current and transfer it to the external load circuit without twisting the wires.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q66: State the working principle of an electrical Transformer.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A Transformer works on the principle of <b>Mutual Induction</b>. When an alternating voltage is applied to the primary coil, the alternating current produces a changing magnetic flux in the common soft iron core, which links with the secondary coil and induces an alternating EMF of the same frequency.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q67: Distinguish between a Step-Up Transformer and a Step-Down Transformer (3 points).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Turn Ratio:</b> Step-up has N<sub>s</sub> &gt; N<sub>p</sub> (K &gt; 1); Step-down has N<sub>s</sub> &lt; N<sub>p</sub> (K &lt; 1).<br>2. <b>Voltage:</b> Step-up increases voltage (V<sub>s</sub> &gt; V<sub>p</sub>); Step-down decreases voltage (V<sub>s</sub> &lt; V<sub>p</sub>).<br>3. <b>Current:</b> Step-up decreases current (I<sub>s</sub> &lt; I<sub>p</sub>); Step-down increases current (I<sub>s</sub> &gt; I<sub>p</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q68: Why can a transformer not be used to step up or step down direct current (DC)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Direct current (DC) produces a steady, constant magnetic field (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt = 0). Since there is no change in magnetic flux linked with the secondary coil, the induced secondary EMF is strictly zero (&Epsilon;<sub>s</sub> = 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q69: A step-down transformer operates on a 2.5 kV primary line to supply a load of 20 A at 220 V. If efficiency is 90%, calculate the primary current.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Output Power: P<sub>out</sub> = V<sub>s</sub> I<sub>s</sub> = 220 &times; 20 = 4400 W.<br>2. Efficiency: &eta; = P<sub>out</sub> / P<sub>in</sub> = 0.90 &rArr; P<sub>in</sub> = 4400 / 0.90 = 4888.89 W.<br>3. Primary Current: I<sub>p</sub> = P<sub>in</sub> / V<sub>p</sub> = 4888.89 / 2500 = <b>1.96 A</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q70: Explain Hysteresis Loss in a transformer and how it is minimized.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      During each cycle of AC, the magnetic domains in the transformer iron core are repeatedly magnetized, demagnetized, and reversed. The energy dissipated as heat due to internal magnetic friction is called <b>Hysteresis Loss</b>. It is minimized by selecting <b>soft iron or silicon steel</b> having a narrow hysteresis loop.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q71: Explain Copper Loss in a transformer and how it is minimized.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Copper loss is the energy dissipated as Joule heat (I<sup>2</sup> R) in the primary and secondary copper windings. It is minimized by using <b>thick copper wires</b> of large cross-sectional area (especially in the low-voltage, high-current winding).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q72: Why does the core of a transformer get warm even when no load is connected to the secondary winding?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Even on open circuit (no load), the primary winding draws a small alternating magnetizing current that continuously magnetizes and demagnetizes the core, producing <b>Hysteresis Loss</b> and circulating <b>Eddy Current Losses</b> in the core iron.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q73: Why is electrical power transmitted at high voltages over long distances?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Transmitted power is P = V I &rArr; I = P / V. By stepping up voltage V to very high levels (e.g. 132 kV or 400 kV), the transmission line current I becomes extremely small. The power loss in transmission cables (P<sub>loss</sub> = I<sup>2</sup> R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P<sup>2</sup> R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sup>2</sup></span></span>) decreases inversely with the square of voltage (1/V<sup>2</sup>), minimizing energy waste.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q74: What is the phase difference between current and voltage in a series LCR circuit at resonance?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      At resonance, X<sub>L</sub> = X<sub>C</sub> &rArr; tan &phi; = (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)/R = 0 &rArr; <b>&phi; = 0&deg;</b>. The alternating voltage and current are <b>perfectly in phase</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q75: Can a capacitor of capacitance C be used as a choke coil in an AC circuit? Explain.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Yes, a capacitor offers reactance X<sub>C</sub> = 1/&omega;C and has zero resistance (R = 0). Its power factor is cos 90&deg; = 0, so it limits AC current without consuming average power. However, unlike an inductive choke whose reactance increases with frequency (X<sub>L</sub> = &omega;L), capacitive reactance drops at higher frequencies (X<sub>C</sub> = 1/&omega;C), which can allow high-frequency noise to pass.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q76: A series LCR circuit with R = 20 &Omega;, L = 1.5 H, and C = 35 &mu;F is connected to a variable-frequency 200 V AC supply. When the frequency of the supply equals the natural frequency of the circuit, what is the average power transferred to the circuit in one complete cycle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      At resonance (supply frequency = natural frequency), impedance is minimum: Z = R = 20 &Omega;, and power factor is cos&phi; = 1.<br>RMS Current: I<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> / R = 200 / 20 = 10 A.<br>Average Power: P<sub>avg</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> I<sub>rms</sub> cos&phi; = 200 &times; 10 &times; 1 = <b>2000 Watts (2.0 kW)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q77: Why is the spark produced in an electric switch more intense when the switch is OPENED (turned off) than when it is CLOSED (turned on)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      When a switch is opened, the current drops from a steady value to zero in an extremely tiny fraction of a second (dt &rarr; 0). This massive rate of current decay (dI/dt &rarr; &infin;) induces an enormous <b>break EMF (&Epsilon; = - L dI/dt)</b> across the opening switch contacts, ionizing the air and creating a brilliant electric spark.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q78: What is the physical meaning of self-inductance being called 'electrical inertia'?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In classical mechanics, mass m resists any change in velocity (F = m a). In electromagnetism, self-inductance L resists any change in electric current (&Epsilon; = - L dI/dt). It opposes current growth when switched on and opposes current decay when switched off.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q79: A radio can tune over the frequency range of a portion of MW broadcast band (800 kHz to 1200 kHz). If its LC circuit has an effective inductance of 200 &mu;H, what must be the range of its variable capacitor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Formula: f = 1 / [2&pi;&radic;(LC)] &rArr; C = 1 / [4&pi;<sup>2</sup> f<sup>2</sup> L].<br>1. For f<sub>max</sub> = 1200 kHz = 1.2 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> Hz:<br>C<sub>min</sub> = 1 / [4&pi;<sup>2</sup> &times; (1.2 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)<sup>2</sup> &times; (200 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>)] = <b>87.9 pF</b>.<br>2. For f<sub>min</sub> = 800 kHz = 0.8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> Hz:<br>C<sub>max</sub> = 1 / [4&pi;<sup>2</sup> &times; (0.8 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>)<sup>2</sup> &times; (200 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>)] = <b>197.8 pF</b>.<br>Capacitor tuning range: <b>87.9 pF to 197.8 pF</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q80: An inductor L, a capacitor C, and a bulb are connected in series to an AC source. How will the brightness of the bulb change if: (a) an iron core is inserted into the inductor, (b) capacitance C is increased such that &omega;L = 1/&omega;C?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      (a) Inserting an iron core increases self-inductance L, which increases inductive reactance X<sub>L</sub> = &omega;L and total impedance Z. Current decreases, so bulb brightness <b>decreases</b>.<br>(b) When &omega;L = 1/&omega;C (resonance condition), total impedance becomes minimum (Z = R) and current reaches its maximum possible value. Therefore, bulb brightness <b>increases to its maximum</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #0091EA; border-bottom: 2px solid #0091EA; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION C: LONG ANSWER DERIVATIONS (5 MARKS EACH &ndash; Q81 TO Q100)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q81: Derive the comprehensive expression for the induced EMF in a straight conductor of length l moving with uniform velocity v in a uniform magnetic field B, and show that mechanical power equals electrical power dissipated.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Geometry &amp; Magnetic Flux:</b> Consider a rectangular conducting frame of width l in a uniform perpendicular magnetic field B. A sliding rod PQ moves to the left with velocity v = - dx/dt.<br>Magnetic flux linked at position x: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B &middot; (l x).<br><b>2. Faraday's Law Induced EMF:</b><br>&Epsilon; = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> (B l x) = - B l (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>) = <b>B l v</b>.<br><b>3. Induced Current:</b> I = &Epsilon; / R = (B l v) / R.<br><b>4. Opposing Magnetic Force:</b> The magnetic Lorentz force on rod is F = I l B = [(B l v)/R] l B = (B<sup>2</sup> l<sup>2</sup> v) / R.<br><b>5. Mechanical Power Applied:</b> P<sub>mech</sub> = F &middot; v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sup>2</sup> l<sup>2</sup> v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span>.<br><b>6. Electrical Joule Heat Rate:</b> P<sub>elec</sub> = I<sup>2</sup> R = [(B l v)/R]<sup>2</sup> R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sup>2</sup> l<sup>2</sup> v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span>.<br><b>Conclusion:</b> P<sub>mech</sub> = P<sub>elec</sub>, strictly verifying Conservation of Energy.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q82: Derive the expression for the self-inductance of a long straight solenoid, and derive the expression for the magnetic energy stored per unit volume (energy density).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Part 1: Self-Inductance:</b><br>1. Solenoid of length l, area A, turns N (n = N/l).<br>2. Internal field: B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N/l) I.<br>3. Flux through each turn: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N/l) I A.<br>4. Total linkage: N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = [(&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A) / l] I.<br>5. L = N &Phi;<sub>B</sub> / I = <b>(&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sup>2</sup> A) / l = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l</b>.<br><br><b>Part 2: Magnetic Energy Density:</b><br>1. Total stored energy: U<sub>B</sub> = 1/2 L I<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 [&mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> A l] I<sup>2</sup>.<br>2. Solenoid volume: V = A l.<br>3. Energy density: u<sub>B</sub> = U<sub>B</sub> / V = 1/2 &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sup>2</sup> I<sup>2</sup>.<br>4. Since B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I &rArr; n I = B / &mu;<sub>0</sub>:<br><b>u<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span></b> [J m<sup>-3</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q83: Derive the expression for the mutual inductance of two long coaxial solenoids of same length l, inner radius r<sub>1</sub>, outer radius r<sub>2</sub>, and turn numbers N<sub>1</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Setup:</b> Solenoid S<sub>1</sub> (inner, radius r<sub>1</sub>, turns N<sub>1</sub>) is placed coaxially inside Solenoid S<sub>2</sub> (outer, radius r<sub>2</sub>, turns N<sub>2</sub>) of common length l.<br><b>2. Current in Outer Solenoid:</b> Let current I<sub>2</sub> flow through S<sub>2</sub>. The uniform magnetic field inside S<sub>2</sub> is:<br>B<sub>2</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sub>2</sub> I<sub>2</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub> / l) I<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>3. Flux Linked with Inner Solenoid S<sub>1</sub>:</b> Since radius of S<sub>1</sub> is r<sub>1</sub>, the cross-sectional area enclosed by S<sub>1</sub> is A<sub>1</sub> = &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>&Phi;<sub>12</sub> = B<sub>2</sub> A<sub>1</sub> = [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub> / l) I<sub>2</sub>] (&pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>).<br><b>4. Total Flux Linkage for N<sub>1</sub> turns:</b><br>N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = N<sub>1</sub> [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (N<sub>2</sub> / l) I<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>] = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span>] I<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>5. Mutual Inductance M:</b> Comparing with N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = M I<sub>2</sub> yields:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>M = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">l</span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n<sub>1</sub> n<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> l</b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q84: Derive the expression for the Root Mean Square (RMS) value of alternating current in terms of peak current I<sub>0</sub>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Definition:</b> RMS current is that steady current which produces the same heat in resistor R in time T as the AC does.<br><b>2. Instantaneous Heat:</b> dH = i<sup>2</sup> R dt = (I<sub>0</sub> sin &omega;t)<sup>2</sup> R dt = I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R sin<sup>2</sup>(&omega;t) dt.<br><b>3. Total Heat over Period T:</b> Using sin<sup>2</sup>(&omega;t) = (1 - cos 2&omega;t) / 2:<br>H = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1 - cos 2&omega;t</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>] dt = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> [&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> dt - &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> cos(2&omega;t) dt].<br>Since &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> cos(2&omega;t) dt = 0, we obtain: H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br><b>4. Equivalent DC Heat:</b> H = I<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> R T.<br><b>5. Equating:</b> I<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> R T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> R T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>I<sub>rms</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;2</span></span> &approx; 0.707 I<sub>0</sub></b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q85: Derive the expression for the impedance, current, and phase angle of a series LCR circuit using the Phasor Diagram method.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Circuit Setup:</b> Resistor R, inductor L, and capacitor C in series across V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t). Let current be I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t &plusmn; &phi;).<br><b>2. Voltage Components:</b><br>• V<sub>R</sub> = I<sub>0</sub> R in phase with current (along x-axis).<br>• V<sub>L</sub> = I<sub>0</sub> X<sub>L</sub> leading current by 90&deg; (along +y axis).<br>• V<sub>C</sub> = I<sub>0</sub> X<sub>C</sub> lagging current by 90&deg; (along -y axis).<br><b>3. Net Reactive Voltage:</b> V<sub>L</sub> - V<sub>C</sub> = I<sub>0</sub> (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>) along +y axis.<br><b>4. Resultant Voltage Phasor V<sub>0</sub>:</b> Applying Pythagoras theorem:<br>V<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = V<sub>R</sub><sup>2</sup> + (V<sub>L</sub> - V<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup> = (I<sub>0</sub> R)<sup>2</sup> + [I<sub>0</sub> (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)]<sup>2</sup> = I<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> [R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>].<br><b>5. Total Impedance Z:</b><br><span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>Z = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub></span></span> = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub>)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (&omega;L - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;C</span></span>)<sup>2</sup>]</b></span>.<br><b>6. Phase Angle &phi;:</b> <b>tan &phi; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">X<sub>L</sub> - X<sub>C</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;L - 1/&omega;C</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q86: Explain Electrical Resonance in a series LCR circuit. Derive the expression for resonant frequency and Quality factor (Q).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Resonance Condition:</b> In a series LCR circuit, resonance occurs when inductive reactance equals capacitive reactance:<br>X<sub>L</sub> = X<sub>C</sub> &rArr; &omega;<sub>r</sub> L = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;<sub>r</sub> C</span></span> &rArr; &omega;<sub>r</sub><sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">LC</span></span> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>&omega;<sub>r</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(LC)</span></span></b></span>.<br>Resonant frequency: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>f<sub>r</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi;&radic;(LC)</span></span></b></span>.<br><b>2. Characteristics:</b> Impedance is minimum Z<sub>min</sub> = R; Current is maximum I<sub>max</sub> = V/R; Phase angle &phi; = 0; Power factor cos&phi; = 1.<br><b>3. Quality Factor (Q):</b> Defined as voltage magnification across L or C at resonance:<br>Q = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>L</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I &omega;<sub>r</sub> L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I R</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;<sub>r</sub> L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(LC)</span></span> &middot; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> = <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span> &radic;<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">C</span></span></b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q87: Derive the expression for the average power consumed over a complete cycle in an alternating current circuit containing resistance, inductance, and capacitance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Instantaneous Voltage &amp; Current:</b><br>v(t) = V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t), &nbsp; i(t) = I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t - &phi;).<br><b>2. Instantaneous Power:</b><br>p(t) = v(t) i(t) = V<sub>0</sub> I<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) sin(&omega;t - &phi;).<br>Using identity 2 sin A sin B = cos(A - B) - cos(A + B):<br>p(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub> I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> [cos &phi; - cos(2&omega;t - &phi;)].<br><b>3. Average Power over Full Period T:</b><br>P<sub>avg</sub> = (1/T) &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> p(t) dt = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub> I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> cos &phi; - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub> I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 T</span></span> &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>T</sup> cos(2&omega;t - &phi;) dt.<br>The integral of the double-frequency cosine term over full cycle is zero.<br><b>4. Final Formula:</b><br>P<sub>avg</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;2</span></span>) (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;2</span></span>) cos &phi; = <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>V<sub>rms</sub> I<sub>rms</sub> cos &phi;</b></span>.<br>Here, <b>cos &phi; = R / Z</b> is the <b>Power Factor</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q88: Describe the construction, working principle, and theory of an AC Generator (Alternator) with a neat mathematical derivation for the induced EMF.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Principle:</b> Converts mechanical energy into electrical energy based on Electromagnetic Induction.<br><b>2. Construction:</b> (a) Armature coil of N turns, (b) Strong permanent/electromagnet field poles, (c) Two slip rings R<sub>1</sub>, R<sub>2</sub>, (d) Two carbon brushes B<sub>1</sub>, B<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>3. Theory &amp; Derivation:</b><br>• When coil of area A rotates at angular velocity &omega;, angle with <b>B</b> at time t is &theta; = &omega;t.<br>• Magnetic flux: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A cos(&omega;t).<br>• Applying Faraday's law: &Epsilon; = - N (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt) = - N (d/dt)[B A cos(&omega;t)] = - N B A (- &omega; sin &omega;t).<br>• Induced EMF: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>&Epsilon; = N B A &omega; sin(&omega;t) = &Epsilon;<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</b></span>,<br>where peak EMF is <b>&Epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = N B A &omega; = 2&pi; f N B A</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q89: Describe the construction, principle, and theory of an Electrical Transformer. Derive the relationship between primary and secondary voltages and currents.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Principle:</b> Mutual Induction between primary and secondary coils wound on a common soft iron core.<br><b>2. Construction:</b> Laminated silicon steel core with Primary winding (N<sub>p</sub> turns) and Secondary winding (N<sub>s</sub> turns).<br><b>3. Working Theory:</b><br>• Applied primary voltage: V<sub>p</sub> = - N<sub>p</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub> / dt).<br>• Induced secondary voltage: V<sub>s</sub> = - N<sub>s</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub> / dt).<br>• Dividing gives: <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>p</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>p</sub></span></span> = K</b> (Transformation Ratio).<br><b>4. Current Relation (Ideal Transformer &eta; = 100%):</b><br>Input Power = Output Power &rArr; V<sub>p</sub> I<sub>p</sub> = V<sub>s</sub> I<sub>s</sub> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>p</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>s</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V<sub>p</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>s</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N<sub>p</sub></span></span> = K</b></span>.<br>• If K &gt; 1 (Step-up): V<sub>s</sub> &gt; V<sub>p</sub> and I<sub>s</sub> &lt; I<sub>p</sub>.<br>• If K &lt; 1 (Step-down): V<sub>s</sub> &lt; V<sub>p</sub> and I<sub>s</sub> &gt; I<sub>p</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q90: Explain the 5 major energy losses occurring in practical transformers and the design methods used to minimize each loss.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Copper Loss (I<sup>2</sup>R Loss):</b> Joule heating in copper wire windings. <i>Remedy:</i> Use thick copper conductors of low resistance.<br><b>2. Iron / Eddy Current Loss:</b> Induced eddy currents in the core dissipating heat. <i>Remedy:</i> Use laminated soft iron core sheets insulated with varnish.<br><b>3. Hysteresis Loss:</b> Energy lost in cyclic domain magnetization of core. <i>Remedy:</i> Use silicon steel core with narrow hysteresis loop.<br><b>4. Flux Leakage:</b> Some primary flux escapes without linking to secondary. <i>Remedy:</i> Wind primary and secondary coils coaxially over each other.<br><b>5. Humming Loss (Magnetostriction):</b> Mechanical vibration noise of core laminations. <i>Remedy:</i> Firmly clamp and bolt the core sheets tightly together.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q91: Derive the expression for the resonant frequency of an LCR series circuit and explain the sharpness of resonance and selectivity.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Net impedance: Z = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + (&omega;L - 1/&omega;C)<sup>2</sup>].<br>2. For maximum current, Z must be minimum &rArr; &omega;L = 1/&omega;C &rArr; &omega;<sub>r</sub> = 1/&radic;(LC) &rArr; <b>f<sub>r</sub> = 1 / [2&pi;&radic;(LC)]</b>.<br>3. Sharpness of resonance is governed by bandwidth 2&Delta;&omega; = &omega;<sub>2</sub> - &omega;<sub>1</sub> = R/L.<br>4. Q-Factor: <b>Q = &omega;<sub>r</sub> / (2&Delta;&omega;) = &omega;<sub>r</sub> L / R = (1/R) &radic;(L/C)</b>.<br>Lower resistance R yields smaller bandwidth, sharper resonant peak, and superior tuning selectivity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q92: Explain the concept of Wattless Current with a phasor diagram and state its practical application in fluorescent tube lights.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. In an AC circuit, current phasor I is resolved into two rectangular components:<br>• <b>In-phase component I<sub>rms</sub> cos &phi;</b> (parallel to V): Consumes active power P = V<sub>rms</sub> (I<sub>rms</sub> cos&phi;) cos 0&deg; = V<sub>rms</sub> I<sub>rms</sub> cos&phi;.<br>• <b>Quadrature component I<sub>rms</sub> sin &phi;</b> (perpendicular to V): Consumes power P = V<sub>rms</sub> (I<sub>rms</sub> sin&phi;) cos 90&deg; = 0. This is the <b>Wattless Current</b>.<br>2. <b>Application:</b> A Choke Coil (high L, R &approx; 0) uses this wattless current principle to choke/limit current in fluorescent tube lights with virtually zero electrical power waste.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q93: A 100 &mu;F capacitor in series with a 40 &Omega; resistance is connected to a 110 V, 60 Hz supply. (a) What is the maximum current in the circuit? (b) What is the time lag between maximum current and maximum voltage?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: C = 100 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> F, R = 40 &Omega;, V<sub>rms</sub> = 110 V, f = 60 Hz.<br>1. X<sub>C</sub> = 1 / (2&pi; f C) = 1 / [2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 60 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup>] = 26.53 &Omega;.<br>2. Impedance Z = &radic;[R<sup>2</sup> + X<sub>C</sub><sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[40<sup>2</sup> + 26.53<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[1600 + 703.8] = &radic;2303.8 = 48.0 &Omega;.<br>3. Peak Voltage: V<sub>0</sub> = &radic;2 &times; 110 = 155.56 V.<br>4. Peak Current: I<sub>0</sub> = V<sub>0</sub> / Z = 155.56 / 48.0 = <b>3.24 A</b>.<br>5. Phase Angle: tan&phi; = X<sub>C</sub> / R = 26.53 / 40 = 0.663 &rArr; &phi; = 33.56&deg; = 0.586 rad.<br>6. Time Lead: &Delta;t = &phi; / &omega; = 0.586 / (2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 60) = <b>1.55 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> s (1.55 ms)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q94: Derive the expression for the induced EMF when a circular coil of N turns and radius R is rotated at constant angular speed &omega; about its diameter in a uniform magnetic field B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Area of circular coil: A = &pi; R<sup>2</sup>.<br>2. At time t, normal to coil makes angle &theta; = &omega;t with magnetic field B.<br>3. Magnetic flux: &Phi;<sub>B</sub> = B A cos(&omega;t) = B (&pi; R<sup>2</sup>) cos(&omega;t).<br>4. Applying Faraday's law: &Epsilon; = - N (d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt) = - N B (&pi; R<sup>2</sup>) (- &omega; sin &omega;t).<br>5. Induced EMF: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>&Epsilon; = N B &pi; R<sup>2</sup> &omega; sin(&omega;t) = &Epsilon;<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</b></span>,<br>where peak EMF is <b>&Epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = N B &pi; R<sup>2</sup> &omega;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q95: A series LCR circuit with L = 3.0 H, C = 27 &mu;F, and R = 7.4 &Omega; is connected to a 230 V variable frequency AC supply. Find (a) resonant frequency, (b) current at resonance, (c) RMS potential drop across the inductor at resonance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: L = 3.0 H, C = 27 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> F, R = 7.4 &Omega;, V<sub>rms</sub> = 230 V.<br>(a) Resonant frequency: &omega;<sub>r</sub> = 1 / &radic;(LC) = 1 / &radic;[3.0 &times; 27 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>] = 1 / &radic;(81 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) = 1 / (9 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) = <b>111.1 rad/s</b> (f<sub>r</sub> = 17.68 Hz).<br>(b) Current at resonance: I<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>rms</sub> / R = 230 / 7.4 = <b>31.08 A</b>.<br>(c) RMS potential drop across inductor: V<sub>L</sub> = I<sub>rms</sub> &times; X<sub>L</sub> = 31.08 &times; (&omega;<sub>r</sub> L) = 31.08 &times; (111.1 &times; 3.0) = 31.08 &times; 333.3 = <b>10,360 Volts (10.36 kV)</b>. (Notice that V<sub>L</sub> is 45 times higher than applied voltage 230 V due to Q-factor magnification!).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q96: Explain the principle of electromagnetic shielding and why a hollow metallic enclosure protects sensitive electrical instruments from external AC magnetic interference.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      When an alternating external magnetic field impinges on a conducting hollow metallic enclosure, circulating eddy currents are induced in the enclosure walls. By Lenz's law, these eddy currents produce their own opposing magnetic fields that cancel the external field in the interior cavity. Thus, sensitive electronic meters placed inside remain completely shielded from external magnetic fluctuations.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q97: Derive the expression for mutual inductance M of two concentric circular planar coils of radii r<sub>1</sub> (inner, small) and r<sub>2</sub> (outer, large) with turn numbers N<sub>1</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Let current I<sub>2</sub> flow in outer large coil of radius r<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub> turns.<br>2. Magnetic field at center: B<sub>2</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>2</sub> I<sub>2</sub>) / (2 r<sub>2</sub>).<br>3. Since inner coil of radius r<sub>1</sub> is very small (r<sub>1</sub> &lt;&lt; r<sub>2</sub>), field B<sub>2</sub> is uniform across its area A<sub>1</sub> = &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>4. Flux linked with inner coil: N<sub>1</sub> &Phi;<sub>12</sub> = N<sub>1</sub> [B<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>] = N<sub>1</sub> [{frac('&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>2</sub> I<sub>2</sub>', '2 r<sub>2</sub>')}] (&pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>) = [{frac('&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>', '2 r<sub>2</sub>')}] I<sub>2</sub>.<br>5. Mutual Inductance: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>M = {frac('&mu;<sub>0</sub> N<sub>1</sub> N<sub>2</sub> &pi; r<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>', '2 r<sub>2</sub>')}</b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q98: Discuss the phenomenon of LC oscillations in an LC tank circuit and show that total electromagnetic energy remains conserved.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Initially, capacitor has charge Q<sub>0</sub> and electrical energy U<sub>E</sub> = Q<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> / (2C) (current i = 0, U<sub>B</sub> = 0).<br>2. As capacitor discharges through inductor L, current grows and energy transfers into magnetic field: U<sub>B</sub> = 1/2 L i<sup>2</sup>.<br>3. Applying loop law: q/C + L (di/dt) = 0 &rArr; {frac('d<sup>2</sup>q', 'dt<sup>2</sup>')} + {frac('1', 'LC')} q = 0 (SHM equation with &omega; = 1/&radic;LC).<br>4. Charge oscillates as q(t) = Q<sub>0</sub> cos(&omega;t), current i(t) = - &omega; Q<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t).<br>5. Total Energy: U<sub>total</sub> = U<sub>E</sub> + U<sub>B</sub> = [q<sup>2</sup>/(2C)] + [1/2 L i<sup>2</sup>] = [Q<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>/(2C)] cos<sup>2</sup>(&omega;t) + 1/2 L (&omega;<sup>2</sup> Q<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>&omega;t) = [Q<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>/(2C)] [cos<sup>2</sup>&omega;t + sin<sup>2</sup>&omega;t] = <b>Q<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> / (2C) = Constant</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q99: A step-up transformer has a turn ratio of 1 : 100. It is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz AC supply. (a) What is the secondary voltage? (b) If the secondary circuit draws a current of 2 A, what is the primary current (assume 100% efficiency)? (c) What is the power delivered to the load?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: V<sub>p</sub> = 220 V, N<sub>p</sub>/N<sub>s</sub> = 1/100 &rArr; K = N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub> = 100, I<sub>s</sub> = 2 A.<br>(a) Secondary Voltage: V<sub>s</sub> = K &times; V<sub>p</sub> = 100 &times; 220 = <b>22,000 Volts (22 kV)</b>.<br>(b) Primary Current: I<sub>p</sub> = K &times; I<sub>s</sub> = 100 &times; 2 = <b>200 Amperes</b>.<br>(c) Power delivered: P = V<sub>s</sub> I<sub>s</sub> = 22000 &times; 2 = <b>44,000 Watts (44 kW)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 145, 234, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #0091EA; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q100: State Faraday's laws of induction and derive the general expression for the motional EMF induced in an arbitrary closed loop moving across a non-uniform magnetic field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Faraday's Law states &Epsilon; = - d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt.<br>2. For a closed loop bounding surface S with line boundary C moving with velocity <b>v</b> in magnetic field <b>B</b>, the total rate of change of flux is:<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = &int;<sub>S</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<b>B</b></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t</span></span> &sdot; d<b>A</b> - &oint;<sub>C</sub> (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>) &sdot; d<b>l</b>.<br>3. For static magnetic field (&part;<b>B</b>/&part;t = 0), the induced motional EMF is strictly the line integral of the magnetic Lorentz force per unit charge around the loop:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>&Epsilon; = &oint;<sub>C</sub> (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>) &sdot; d<b>l</b></b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

